WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.400
SPEAKER 1: Let me show you
how to create your database

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.190
and run migrations using
App Engine Flux in Rails.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.440
I've written an app, and
I'm using the MySQL2 gem

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.660
to connect to my database.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.300
I've created a second generation
cloud SQL instance called

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.826
Rails instance.

00:00:13.826 --> 00:00:15.450
And I set up the user
name and password

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.640
that my Rails app will use.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.980
I've also downloaded
the cloud SQL proxy,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.965
and I have it running
in the background.

00:00:21.965 --> 00:00:25.140
To connect my Rails app
to my cloud SQL instance,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.570
I need to get the
connection name from gcloud.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:30.900
I use gcloud SQL instances
describe and then my instance

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:31.731
name.

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:33.480
And since I just need
the connection name,

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.550
I'll pipe that through grep.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:40.970
I take the connection name and I
can put it in my database.yaml.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.320
I also add the user name and
password while I'm in here.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.830
So as the proxy is running,
I can set my Rails env

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:51.040
to production and I can use
my regular Rails commands

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.990
to run the database create
and database migrations.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.630
Now I'm ready to deploy
my app to App Engine.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.750
I hope this was helpful.

